// imagecodec.nv — the front: `decode` dispatches on the bytes,
// `encode` on a format the caller named, and neither of them guesses.
//
// WHAT A MULTI-FORMAT FRONT IS FOR
//
// A program that opens an image does not usually care which format it
// is; a program that writes one always does.  That asymmetry is the
// whole shape of this module: `decode` takes bytes and answers an
// `Image`, and `encode` takes an `Image` and a format WITH its options,
// because there is no default JPEG quality that is right for both a
// thumbnail and an archive.
//
// THIS PACKAGE DEPENDS ON png-nv AND qoi-nv; IT DOES NOT CONTAIN THEM
//
// The must-have plan gives png-nv "the format on its own" and image-nv
// "the multi-format front", and the honest reading of those two rows is
// a dependency rather than a duplicate.  Two reasons, and the second is
// the one that decides it:
//
//   * Two PNG codecs in one registry is one codec and a copy that is
//     always slightly behind it.  Whichever were fixed first, the other
//     would carry the bug.
//   * **They are not the same package.**  A program that only reads PNG
//     — a favicon service, a plot renderer, a screenshot differ —
//     should not acquire a JPEG decoder, an Adam7 composer and a
//     Lanczos resampler to do it.  png-nv at `core` with two
//     dependencies is a genuinely smaller thing to take than image-nv
//     with five, and the grid exists so that a caller can take the
//     smaller one.
//
// The precedent for the other answer — heapless-nv absorbing the
// ringbuf-nv row — applies where two rows are ONE thing.  These are
// two things, and the test is that a sensible program wants exactly
// one of them.
//
// WHY `encode` REFUSES RATHER THAN CONVERTS
//
// Writing an RGBA image as JPEG has to lose the alpha and writing a
// 16-bit image as QOI has to lose eight bits a channel.  This package
// refuses both, with `ImgFormatCannotCarry` naming what would have been
// lost.  A caller who meant to calls `imageops.convert` first — and the
// conversion is then a line in their code that a reader can see,
// instead of a silence inside a call that looked like it was only
// choosing a container.

use image
use imagefmt
use imageops

// A format to write, with the options for writing it.
//
// A separate type from `ImageFormat` because the two answer different
// questions: `ImageFormat` is what `sniff` returns and carries no
// options, and this is what `encode` takes and carries all of them.
// Pairing each format with its own options in the variant is what
// makes an impossible combination unrepresentable — there is no way to
// hand a JPEG quality to a PNG encoder, because the constructor does
// not admit one.
//
// ```novo
// use imagecodec
// use pngenc
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let e = EncPng(pngenc.default_options())
//     println("${imagecodec.encoding_name(e)}")            // png
// ```
pub enum ImageEncoding
    // PNG, with png-nv's own options: the DEFLATE level and the filter
    // strategy.
    EncPng(options: PngOptions)
    // JPEG, with the quality, the mode and the chroma subsampling.
    EncJpeg(options: JpegOptions)
    // QOI, which has no options at all — the format has no level, no
    // strategy and no window, which is most of why it exists.
    EncQoi

// The format an encoding writes.
//
// ```novo
// use imagecodec
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${imagefmt.format_name(imagecodec.encoding_format(EncQoi))}")   // qoi
// ```
pub fn encoding_format(e: ImageEncoding) -> ImageFormat
    todo("image-nv.imagecodec.encoding_format")

// The name of the format an encoding writes.
//
// ```novo
// use imagecodec
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${imagecodec.encoding_name(EncQoi)}")       // qoi
// ```
pub fn encoding_name(e: ImageEncoding) -> Str
    todo("image-nv.imagecodec.encoding_name")

// The encoding a format's defaults describe.
//
// PNG at level 6 with the full filter search, JPEG at quality 85 with
// 4:2:0 chroma subsampling, QOI as itself.  For the caller who has a
// format and no opinion — a converter honouring an output extension,
// a test.
//
// ```novo
// use imagecodec
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${imagecodec.encoding_name(imagecodec.default_encoding(FmtPng))}")   // png
// ```
pub fn default_encoding(f: ImageFormat) -> ImageEncoding
    todo("image-nv.imagecodec.default_encoding")

// Decode a whole file, dispatching on its magic.
//
// The front door.  The format is read from the bytes and never from a
// name, so a `.png` whose contents are a JPEG decodes as a JPEG — and
// a caller who wanted the name honoured instead is asking for the
// upload vulnerability, which is why that is not what this does.
//
// The resulting `Image` is in whatever `PixelKind` the file's own
// contents map to: an indexed PNG becomes `PixRgba8` or `PixRgb8`
// depending on whether it had a tRNS, a 16-bit PNG stays 16-bit, a
// baseline JPEG becomes `PixRgb8`, and a three-channel QOI becomes
// `PixRgb8`.  `imageops.convert` is where a caller who wants one
// specific kind says so, and `decode_as_kind` is that in one call.
//
// ```novo
// use imagecodec
//
// fn main() [io]
//     match imagecodec.decode(bytes.zeros(0))
//         Ok(_)  => println("decoded")
//         Err(_) => println("not an image")                // this one
// ```
pub fn decode(src: Bytes) -> Result<Image, ImageError>
    todo("image-nv.imagecodec.decode")

// Decode a whole file that is known to be a particular format.
//
// The escape from sniffing, for the caller who has out-of-band
// knowledge — a database column with a format field, a protocol that
// declared it — and takes responsibility for being right.  A file
// whose bytes are a different format fails inside that format's codec
// rather than being recognised, which is the cost of the escape and is
// why `decode` is the default.
//
// ```novo
// use imagecodec
//
// fn main() [io]
//     match imagecodec.decode_as(bytes.zeros(0), FmtPng)
//         Ok(_)  => println("decoded")
//         Err(_) => println("not a PNG")                   // this one
// ```
pub fn decode_as(src: Bytes, f: ImageFormat) -> Result<Image, ImageError>
    todo("image-nv.imagecodec.decode_as")

// Decode a whole file and convert it to a chosen pixel kind.
//
// The call a caller uploading into a texture or a fixed pipeline
// wants: one kind out, whatever went in.  It is exactly `decode`
// followed by `imageops.convert`, written once because the pair is what
// most callers mean.
//
// ```novo
// use imagecodec
//
// fn main() [io]
//     match imagecodec.decode_as_kind(bytes.zeros(0), PixRgba8)
//         Ok(_)  => println("decoded")
//         Err(_) => println("not an image")                // this one
// ```
pub fn decode_as_kind(src: Bytes, k: PixelKind) -> Result<Image, ImageError>
    todo("image-nv.imagecodec.decode_as_kind")

// Read an image's dimensions and format without decoding its pixels.
//
// The question a thumbnailer, an upload handler or a layout engine
// asks first, answered from the header alone — thirty-three bytes for
// a PNG, fourteen for a QOI, however far the first frame header is for
// a JPEG.  No pixel buffer is allocated, so a caller can decide
// whether to commit memory before they do.
//
// ```novo
// use imagecodec
//
// fn main() [io]
//     match imagecodec.read_size(bytes.zeros(0))
//         Ok(_)  => println("read")
//         Err(_) => println("not an image")                // this one
// ```
pub fn read_size(src: Bytes) -> Result<(ImageFormat, Int, Int, PixelKind), ImageError>
    todo("image-nv.imagecodec.read_size")

// Refuse an image larger than a bound.
//
// A decoded image's size is the product of two numbers a FILE chose,
// and both PNG and QOI carry them in 32-bit fields — so a forty-byte
// header can claim an image of four billion by four billion, and the
// compressed data that fills it with zeroes is a few hundred bytes.
// That is the decompression bomb every image decoder is eventually
// handed.
//
// The bound is in BYTES of decoded image, because that is the number a
// caller's memory budget is in.  The default is unbounded, because the
// right bound is the caller's and not this package's opinion; past it
// the decode fails with `ImgImageTooLarge`.
//
// ```novo
// use imagecodec
//
// fn main() [io]
//     match imagecodec.decode_bounded(bytes.zeros(0), 268435456)
//         Ok(_)  => println("decoded")
//         Err(_) => println("refused")                     // this one
// ```
pub fn decode_bounded(src: Bytes, cap: Int) -> Result<Image, ImageError>
    todo("image-nv.imagecodec.decode_bounded")

// Encode an image in a named format with its options.
//
// `Err(ImgFormatCannotCarry)` when the format cannot represent what
// the image has: an alpha channel as JPEG, sixteen-bit samples as QOI.
// The refusal is the design — see this module's header — and
// `imageops.convert` is the one line that answers it.
//
// ```novo
// use imagecodec
// use image
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let img = image.new(2, 2, PixRgb8)
//     match imagecodec.encode(img, EncQoi)
//         Ok(_)  => println("encoded")                     // this one
//         Err(_) => println("refused")
// ```
pub fn encode(img: Image, e: ImageEncoding) -> Result<Bytes, ImageError>
    todo("image-nv.imagecodec.encode")

// Whether an encoding can carry everything an image has.
//
// The question `encode` answers by refusing, asked in advance — for a
// caller choosing a format from several, or one who wants to warn
// before converting rather than after failing.
//
// ```novo
// use imagecodec
// use image
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${imagecodec.can_carry(image.new(2, 2, PixRgba8), EncQoi)}")     // true
// ```
pub fn can_carry(img: Image, e: ImageEncoding) -> Bool
    todo("image-nv.imagecodec.can_carry")

// The pixel kind an encoding needs, for an image it cannot carry as
// it is.
//
// What to pass `imageops.convert` before encoding: `PixRgb8` for JPEG,
// `PixRgba8` or `PixRgb8` for QOI depending on whether the image has
// alpha, and the image's own kind for PNG, which carries all eight.
// It is the other half of `can_carry`: one says no, this says what
// would make it yes.
//
// ```novo
// use imagecodec
// use image
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let img = image.new(2, 2, PixRgba16)
//     println("${image.bytes_per_pixel(imagecodec.kind_for(img, EncQoi))}")     // 4
// ```
pub fn kind_for(img: Image, e: ImageEncoding) -> PixelKind
    todo("image-nv.imagecodec.kind_for")

// Decode a whole image from a stream, charged what the stream costs.
//
// THE ONE EFFECT-POLYMORPHIC SIGNATURE IN THIS PACKAGE.  `S: Read[e]`
// binds the effect parameter of the standard library's `Read` trait
// and the clause is `[e]`, so the row means "whatever the impl behind
// `S` supplies" — a `File` charges its caller `[io, fs]`, an in-memory
// buffer charges nothing, and image-nv remains `core` because it has
// named no effect of its own (SPEC § 5.6).
//
// It sniffs the first `imagefmt.magic_length` bytes and hands the rest
// to the format's own decoder, so a caller streaming a network body
// never holds the compressed file — except for a JPEG, which this
// package cannot stream at all.  The README's JPEG section says why,
// and the short version is progressive mode: a progressive scan's
// coefficients are refined across the whole file, so nothing can be
// output until the last scan has been read.
//
// A stream failure comes back as `ImgSourceFailed` carrying the host's
// own `IoError`.
//
// ```novo
// use imagecodec
// use imageerror
//
// fn read_image(path: Str) -> Result<Image, ImageError> [io, fs]
//     match File.open_read(path)
//         None    => Err(ImgSourceFailed(IoNotFound))
//         Some(f) =>
//             let h: File = f
//             imagecodec.read_all(h)
//
// fn main() [io, fs]
//     match read_image("in.png")
//         Ok(_)  => println("decoded")
//         Err(e) => println(e.message())
// ```
pub fn read_all<S: Read[e]>(src: S) -> Result<Image, ImageError> [e]
    todo("image-nv.imagecodec.read_all")
